Do not create packages merely for architectural symmetry.

Follow normal Go package conventions.

# html/template

Use Go's standard `html/template` package.

Take advantage of its contextual escaping.

Templates should produce the minimum HTML structure required by GOV.UK Frontend.

Do not introduce arbitrary wrapper elements.

Do not change elements simply because another structure is easier to template.

Preserve:

- elements
- element ordering
- classes
- attributes
- IDs
- names
- values
- ARIA relationships
- data attributes
- error markup
- hint markup
- fieldsets
- legends
- conditional content
- nested component structures

Keep template logic straightforward.

Use template actions for normal rendering decisions.

Where rendering logic becomes complicated, perform the transformation in Go and pass a suitable view model to the template.

Do not create large amounts of application logic inside templates.

# Template composition

Use named templates to compose GOV.UK components where appropriate.

For example:

```gotemplate
{{ define "govukButton" }}
<button
  type="{{ .Type }}"
  class="govuk-button{{ with .Classes }} {{ . }}{{ end }}"
  data-module="govuk-button"
>
  {{ .Text }}
</button>
{{ end }}
```

Reusable subcomponents should be composed rather than duplicated.

Examples include:

- labels
- hints
- error messages
- fieldsets
- legends
- form groups

Composition must not introduce differences from expected GOV.UK Frontend HTML.

# Template loading

Parse templates during application startup rather than reparsing them on every request.

Fail application startup clearly if required templates cannot be parsed.

Where practical, consider Go's `embed` package for templates and static resources that belong to the Go application.

Do not make development convenience more important than production correctness.

# Escaping and security

Use `html/template` specifically because it provides contextual HTML escaping.

Ordinary strings must be treated as untrusted.

A GOV.UK Frontend option named `text` must be escaped.

Do not convert normal strings to `template.HTML`.

GOV.UK Frontend options named `html` require an explicit trusted HTML mechanism.

If required, define a small abstraction such as:

```go
type TrustedHTML string
```

Only convert an explicitly trusted value to `template.HTML` at the rendering boundary.

Never convert arbitrary user-controlled input to `template.HTML`.

Never use `template.HTML` merely to make a fixture pass.

Do not disable or bypass Go's normal contextual escaping without a clear, documented reason.

The distinction between text and trusted HTML must be deliberate and testable.

# Template helper functions

Use `template.FuncMap` only where it makes templates clearer.

Template functions must be:

- deterministic
- small
- easy to test
- concerned with rendering

Do not hide substantial application logic inside template functions.

Do not create helpers that return `template.HTML` unless their purpose is specifically to produce controlled trusted markup.

Any helper returning trusted markup requires dedicated security tests.

# Attributes

Support arbitrary GOV.UK Frontend attributes where the upstream component exposes an `attributes` option.

Do not concatenate arbitrary raw attribute strings.

Render attribute names and values safely.

Pay particular attention to:

- `id`
- `name`
- `value`
- `type`
- `role`
- `aria-*`
- `data-*`
- `disabled`
- `checked`
- `selected`
- `autocomplete`
- `for`

Boolean attributes must follow valid HTML semantics.

Attribute handling must be deterministic.

If maps are used internally, do not rely on Go map iteration order where output ordering matters.

Sort attribute keys before rendering if deterministic ordering is required.

# Determinism

Rendering must be deterministic.

The same component options must produce equivalent output on every execution.

Remember that Go deliberately does not guarantee map iteration order.

Never depend on map iteration order for:

- attributes
- classes
- fixture processing
- expected rendering order

Sort keys explicitly where ordering affects deterministic output or testing.

# Fixture models

Use dedicated Go structs for fixture data where practical.

Fixture representations do not have to be identical to the public component API.

Keep fixture parsing concerns separate from component rendering concerns.

Use YAML struct tags appropriately.

For example:

```go
type Fixture struct {
    Name    string         `yaml:"name"`
    Options map[string]any `yaml:"options"`
    HTML    string         `yaml:"html"`
}
```

Prefer stronger typing once the fixture structure is understood.

Avoid spreading `map[string]any` throughout component rendering code.

Convert generic fixture data into strongly typed component options at the fixture boundary.

# Fixture testing

Fixture testing must be data-driven.

For every supported fixture:

1. Load the upstream fixture data.
2. Identify the component and fixture.
3. Deserialize its options.
4. Map the fixture options into the appropriate Go component model.
5. Render the component using `html/template`.
6. Obtain the expected GOV.UK Frontend HTML.
7. Parse the actual HTML.
8. Parse the expected HTML.
9. Compare meaningful DOM structures.
10. Produce a useful diagnostic diff when they differ.

A new upstream fixture should normally be testable without adding a new Go test function.

Use table-driven tests.

For example:

```go
func TestButtonFixtures(t *testing.T) {
    fixtures := loadButtonFixtures(t)

    for _, fixture := range fixtures {
        t.Run(fixture.Name, func(t *testing.T) {
            actual := renderButton(t, fixture.Options)

            assertHTMLEqual(
                t,
                fixture.HTML,
                actual,
            )
        })
    }
}
```

# HTML parsing and comparison

Use `golang.org/x/net/html` or another standards-aware Go HTML parser.

Do not rely exclusively on raw string equality.

Template engines may produce semantically irrelevant differences in whitespace or serialisation.

Normalisation must be conservative.

It may normalise genuinely insignificant differences where HTML semantics are unchanged.

It must not hide differences involving:

- missing elements
- additional elements
- element types
- element ordering
- text content
- attributes
- attribute values
- classes
- IDs
- names
- values
- ARIA attributes
- data attributes
- form semantics
- accessibility relationships

Document every normalisation rule.

Every non-trivial normalisation rule must have tests demonstrating:

1. the irrelevant difference it intentionally ignores
2. meaningful differences it still detects

When a fixture parity test fails, assume the component implementation is incorrect before assuming the comparator is incorrect.

Do not weaken the comparator merely to increase the fixture pass rate.

# Useful failure output

Fixture failures should be easy to diagnose.

Where practical, report:

- fixture name
- component name
- expected structure
- actual structure
- first meaningful difference

Do not require developers to inspect an enormous raw HTML dump to identify a simple missing attribute.

# Testing

Use Go's standard `testing` package.

Prefer table-driven tests.

Use `t.Run()` for named fixture cases.

Use `t.Helper()` in testing helpers.

Tests must cover:

- every supported official fixture
- default options
- optional options
- text escaping
- trusted HTML
- custom classes
- arbitrary attributes
- IDs
- ARIA attributes
- data attributes
- errors
- hints
- nested content
- form values
- checked states
- selected states
- disabled states
- complex components
- comparator behaviour
- fixture parsing

Do not introduce a third-party assertion framework unless it provides a clear benefit.

Prefer standard Go testing where practical.

# HTTP server

Prefer `net/http`.

Use modern standard-library routing capabilities where they meet the application's requirements.

Handlers should be small.

Handlers should:

1. receive and validate HTTP input
2. call application logic
3. construct view/component models
4. render the response

Do not embed substantial GOV.UK component rendering logic directly inside handlers.

# Error handling

Return errors explicitly.

Do not silently ignore template errors.

Do not silently ignore fixture parsing errors.

Wrap errors with useful context where appropriate using `%w`.

For example:

```go
return fmt.Errorf("render govuk button: %w", err)
```

Avoid `panic` in normal request processing.

A startup failure caused by invalid embedded templates may reasonably terminate the application.

Tests should fail clearly when fixture loading or rendering fails.

# Context

Use `context.Context` for request-scoped cancellation, deadlines, and values where appropriate.

Pass context explicitly.

Do not store contexts in structs.

Do not add context parameters to pure component rendering functions unless they genuinely require request-scoped behaviour.

GOV.UK component rendering should normally be deterministic and independent of request context.

# Concurrency

Go provides excellent concurrency primitives, but do not introduce goroutines simply because they are available.

Component rendering normally does not require explicit concurrency.

Use concurrency only where it provides a clear application benefit.

Always consider:

- cancellation
- error propagation
- goroutine lifetime
- shared state
- race conditions

The application must pass race detection where practical.

# Shared state

Avoid mutable global state.

Parsed templates may be safely shared where Go's template concurrency guarantees allow it.

Treat component options as request-specific values.

Do not mutate shared template or component state during rendering.

# Progressive enhancement

The initial HTTP response must contain the complete core content and controls required for the journey.

Use official GOV.UK Frontend JavaScript to progressively enhance components requiring client-side behaviour.

The Go implementation is responsible for producing the correct initial HTML.

Do not use JavaScript to repair incorrectly rendered server-side markup.

Do not make JavaScript responsible for constructing essential initial component content.

# Assets

Use the official GOV.UK Frontend package as the source of:

- CSS
- JavaScript
- fonts
- images
- other distributed assets

Pin the GOV.UK Frontend version.

Provide a reproducible mechanism for obtaining or building those assets.

Node.js may be used as development/build tooling where required to obtain GOV.UK Frontend assets.

Node.js must not participate in application runtime rendering.

Do not silently depend on a CDN version that can change independently of the application.

# Dependencies

Dependency updates, lockfiles, audits, and Dependabot / Renovate review follow [`.cursor/skills/safe-dependency-updates/SKILL.md`](../skills/safe-dependency-updates/SKILL.md). Keep the dependency surface small; prefer platform / standard-library APIs first. Do **not** start or complete dependency changes while CI is red.

# Formatting

All Go source must be formatted using standard Go tooling.

Run:

```bash
gofmt
```

or:

```bash
go fmt ./...
```

Do not introduce custom formatting conventions that conflict with normal Go style.

# Static analysis

Run:

```bash
go vet ./...
```

Use additional established static analysis tooling only where it provides clear value.

Do not introduce large linting configurations merely to enforce subjective style preferences.

# Race detection

Where practical, CI should run:

```bash
go test -race ./...
```

This is particularly useful if the application introduces shared rendering infrastructure or concurrency.

# Code quality

Follow idiomatic Go.

Prefer:

- small packages
- explicit dependencies
- simple structs
- composition
- interfaces defined by consumers when genuinely useful
- table-driven tests
- explicit error handling
- standard-library functionality
- straightforward templates
- clear package names

Avoid:

- Java-style class hierarchies
- unnecessary interfaces
- interfaces with only one implementation unless they provide genuine value
- excessive abstraction
- generic abstractions without a concrete need
- mutable global state
- reflection where normal Go is clearer
- HTML string concatenation
- premature concurrency
- unnecessary JavaScript
- architecture copied directly from TypeScript or another implementation

Do not create getters and setters simply to imitate object-oriented languages.

Do not create an interface solely to make a struct mockable.

Design the Go implementation as Go.

# Performance

Do not prematurely optimise component rendering.

Correctness and fixture parity come first.

However:

- parse templates once rather than once per request
- avoid unnecessary allocations where obvious
- avoid unnecessary reflection
- do not perform filesystem template discovery on every request
- keep request paths simple

Benchmark only where there is a meaningful performance question.

Use Go benchmarks where appropriate:

```go
func BenchmarkButtonRender(b *testing.B) {
    // ...
}
```

Do not sacrifice readability or correctness for speculative micro-optimisation.

# Accessibility

Accessibility-related markup is part of the compatibility contract.

Preserve expected:

- semantic HTML
- labels
- legends
- fieldsets
- IDs
- `for` relationships
- hint associations
- error associations
- `aria-describedby`
- `aria-labelledby`
- roles
- live regions
- visually hidden content

Do not change accessibility markup because an alternative appears equivalent without first confirming that it remains compatible with the GOV.UK Frontend contract.

Fixture parity does not replace wider accessibility testing.

The resulting example service should still follow WCAG 2.2 AA and GOV.UK accessibility guidance.

# Forbidden shortcuts

Never:

- modify an upstream fixture to make a test pass
- modify expected HTML to match the Go implementation
- special-case an individual fixture by name
- hard-code expected fixture output
- copy pre-rendered fixture HTML into a component
- weaken the comparator to hide a genuine difference
- invoke Nunjucks at application runtime
- invoke Node.js to generate production component HTML
- call another GOV.UK implementation at runtime
- use React, Vue, Angular, Svelte, or another client-side framework to construct core GOV.UK component markup
- replace GOV.UK Frontend CSS
- unnecessarily replace GOV.UK Frontend JavaScript
- bypass `html/template` escaping to make tests pass
- use JavaScript to repair server-rendered fixture incompatibilities

If a fixture cannot currently be implemented correctly, leave the limitation visible and document it rather than cheating the compatibility test.

# CI

CI must at minimum run:

```bash
go fmt ./...
go vet ./...
go test ./...
```

Where practical also run:

```bash
go test -race ./...
govulncheck ./...
```

CI must execute the complete GOV.UK Frontend fixture parity suite.

Any fixture parity failure must fail CI.

# GOV.UK Frontend upgrades

Treat a GOV.UK Frontend upgrade as a compatibility event.

When upgrading:

1. Update the pinned GOV.UK Frontend version.
2. Obtain the fixtures belonging to that exact version.
3. Do not modify the Go implementation initially.
4. Run the complete existing fixture suite against the new fixtures.
5. Inspect failures.
6. Determine whether failures represent legitimate upstream changes.
7. Update Go component models and templates where necessary.
8. Rerun the complete suite.
9. Update documentation where the public component API changes.

Do not modify new fixtures to preserve old behaviour.

Fixture failures during an upgrade are useful because they identify exactly where the independent implementation has diverged from the new GOV.UK Frontend contract.

# Definition of done

A GOV.UK component is complete only when:

- it is server-rendered using Go and `html/template`
- it is independently renderable from an HTTP handler
- it has no Nunjucks runtime rendering dependency
- it has no Node.js runtime rendering dependency
- it uses official GOV.UK Frontend assets
- every supported upstream fixture passes
- ordinary text escaping is tested
- trusted HTML behaviour is tested
- arbitrary attributes are tested
- accessibility-related markup matches expected output
- the DOM comparator has not been weakened
- no fixture-specific hacks exist
- Go formatting and static analysis pass
- all tests pass
- CI passes

The primary success metric is fixture parity, not visual similarity.

The implementation should demonstrate that idiomatic Go can independently produce the same GOV.UK Frontend HTML contract as the reference implementation without requiring a frontend framework.
